WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.738
Case studies, part two: disaster science.

00:00:04.098 --> 00:00:08.767
We apply the same equation to flood
and heat risk in three African cities:

00:00:09.017 --> 00:00:14.307
Lilongwe in Malawi, Lagos in
Nigeria, and Bamako in Mali.

00:00:14.838 --> 00:00:21.153
A reminder before we start. RMTI is
a research prototype: not validated,

00:00:21.256 --> 00:00:25.626
not a medical device, and not for
patient-care or emergency decisions.

00:00:25.941 --> 00:00:30.618
Everything here uses illustrative values,
published rows, or aggregate counts.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:35.403
No patient data appears. Here is
how the inputs map to a city,

00:00:35.403 --> 00:00:38.333
using the RMTI indicator backbone.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:42.971
Exposure is raised by hazards,
climate change and urbanisation.

00:00:43.401 --> 00:00:47.251
Vulnerability is raised by
poverty, governance and pollution.

00:00:47.666 --> 00:00:51.415
Resilience comes from risk-management
strategy, infrastructure,

00:00:51.489 --> 00:00:55.803
and health and water security. What
can each lever mean on the ground?

00:00:56.153 --> 00:01:00.210
These are illustrative examples,
not evidence-based effect sizes.

00:01:00.574 --> 00:01:04.921
For flood: keep people out of the
floodplain, which acts on exposure;

00:01:05.269 --> 00:01:09.397
improve housing and keep services
running, which acts on vulnerability;

00:01:09.737 --> 00:01:13.502
and build early warning, drainage
and emergency health capacity,

00:01:13.583 --> 00:01:16.294
which raises resilience. For heat:

00:01:16.565 --> 00:01:19.422
reduce the share of people in
the hottest neighbourhoods,

00:01:19.591 --> 00:01:24.033
improve housing and tackle energy
poverty, and build cooling centres,

00:01:24.033 --> 00:01:26.337
heat-health plans and reliable power.

00:01:26.811 --> 00:01:30.824
Published rubric values from the
disaster calculator: Lilongwe,

00:01:31.058 --> 00:01:36.747
pluvial and river flood; Lagos,
coastal and pluvial flood; and Bamako,

00:01:36.998 --> 00:01:40.197
a multi-hazard case of
drought, flood and heat.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:44.256
These are rubric scores, not
damage or casualty estimates.

00:01:44.662 --> 00:01:46.582
Each city travels the cube.

00:01:46.898 --> 00:01:51.050
All three combine high likelihood
with high exposure and vulnerability.

00:01:51.354 --> 00:01:55.490
With today’s resilience, all
three land in tier 4, severe:

00:01:55.862 --> 00:02:01.091
R of 0.42 for Lilongwe, 0.37 for Lagos,

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:05.977
and 0.41 for Bamako. Under
scenario resilience targets,

00:02:06.105 --> 00:02:11.064
all three fall to tier 3, high.
These after values are targets,

00:02:11.064 --> 00:02:15.826
not observed results. Lever
one, resilience, for Lilongwe.

00:02:16.175 --> 00:02:19.091
The calculator’s target pathway is 0.43

00:02:19.091 --> 00:02:23.324
to 0.53 by 2027 to 2030.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:26.062
R drops below the severe threshold of

00:02:26.062 --> 00:02:30.487
0.35 once resilience passes about 0.38,

00:02:30.663 --> 00:02:33.097
and the target pathway
goes well beyond it.

00:02:33.483 --> 00:02:37.670
Lever two, exposure, shows
why inputs must be audited.

00:02:37.975 --> 00:02:42.508
For Lagos the published
rubric uses exposure 0.65,

00:02:42.712 --> 00:02:46.384
but the population-based
helper gives about 0.98.

00:02:46.647 --> 00:02:48.724
Same city, same equation:

00:02:49.032 --> 00:02:53.452
R would rise from 0.37 to 0.56,

00:02:53.637 --> 00:02:56.031
still severe but close to critical.

00:02:56.454 --> 00:03:00.981
Sensitivity like this is exactly what
an auditable index should expose.

00:03:01.505 --> 00:03:07.453
Lever three, vulnerability, for Bamako,
where drought, flood and heat combine.

00:03:07.798 --> 00:03:12.570
At vulnerability 0.75, R is 0.41.

00:03:12.943 --> 00:03:14.089
Housing quality,

00:03:14.089 --> 00:03:17.921
service continuity and support for
the poorest would move this lever.

00:03:18.312 --> 00:03:24.054
R drops below 0.35 once
vulnerability falls to about 0.64.

00:03:24.515 --> 00:03:28.693
For Bamako, which single lever
would be enough to leave tier 4?

00:03:29.156 --> 00:03:35.397
Resilience at least 0.31;
vulnerability at most 0.64;

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:39.023
exposure at most 0.73;

00:03:39.425 --> 00:03:42.690
or likelihood at most 0.69.

00:03:43.157 --> 00:03:47.335
Likelihood is the hardest to move,
because it is set largely by climate.

00:03:47.736 --> 00:03:52.128
Realistic plans combine levers.
Side by side for Bamako,

00:03:52.262 --> 00:03:58.454
each lever moved by ten points, from largest
to smallest: vulnerability, resilience,

00:03:58.564 --> 00:04:03.818
likelihood and exposure. The grey
bar is likelihood: it moves R,

00:04:03.818 --> 00:04:08.732
but a city cannot choose it. The teal
bars are the levers a programme can pull.

00:04:09.099 --> 00:04:11.265
What this shows and does not show.

00:04:11.725 --> 00:04:18.097
The arithmetic and the levers are RMTI’s
own, applied to published rubric values.

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:23.362
The after values are scenario targets,
R is a relative rubric index,

00:04:23.498 --> 00:04:28.072
and none of this is a forecast, an
engineering assessment, or a loss estimate.

00:04:28.372 --> 00:04:32.232
Investment decisions need local
data and professional assessment.

00:04:32.618 --> 00:04:38.337
That is RMTI on flood
and heat: one equation,

00:04:38.337 --> 00:04:42.368
four levers, and a clear view of what
each would take to leave a tier.

00:04:42.777 --> 00:04:45.469
I’m Dr. Cesar Marolla.

00:04:45.725 --> 00:04:47.817
The code is open under Apache 2.0,
the demo uses no patient data,

00:04:47.952 --> 00:04:50.273
and nothing here is engineering advice.

